From: quickjs-ng bundle work
Subject: Deferred RC: mark parked objects so the register validator is not a false positive (0191)

What this does
  `js_drc_zero` parks a zero-count object on `gc_zero_ref_count_list` but did
  not set the `in_zct` marker on it, while a parked string or BigInt did set
  it. The register validator (`QJS_DRC_VALIDATE`) uses that marker to tell a
  borrowing register that still names a parked value from one that names
  garbage, so it aborted on every box2d/deltablue/richards run with
  `register ... holds a counted value at ref_count 0`. The same marker is what
  `JS_DRC_UNIQUELY_OWNED` reads to refuse in-place mutation of a value that is
  parked and only resurrected.

Why we need it
  Registers borrow under deferred RC, so a counted value at ref_count 0 in a
  register is normal: it is parked for a deferred free. The validator's rule was
  the owning-register invariant and needs the parked marker to be honest. With
  the marker set on objects too, the validator passes on the baseline, which is
  what a change that removes a reference-count pair (S3) needs to be checked
  against.

Measured
  box2d `check.js` 546.1008391043414 under `-DQJS_DRC_VALIDATE=1`;
  deltablue and richards run to completion; box2d `fixed.js` instruction count
  unchanged (21,806.1M -> 21,576.5M with 0190, same as before this patch).

No bytecode change:
  No. Runtime only.

diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
index c0e2e3e..8972d49 100644
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -905,7 +905,22 @@ static void js_drc_validate_range(void *opaque, JSValue *regs, uint32_t nregs)
         JSValue v = regs[i];
         if (!JS_VALUE_HAS_REF_COUNT(v))
             continue;
+        /*
+         * Registers borrow under deferred RC (stage D4): the value they hold is
+         * owned elsewhere, or -- when its count has already reached zero -- is
+         * parked on the zero-count table, which keeps it alive until the
+         * deferred free runs. So a counted value at count zero is legitimate
+         * *if it is parked*. Only a value that is counted, at zero and not
+         * parked is unreachable garbage: nothing will free it and nothing owns
+         * it. With owning registers (the default before D4) the original,
+         * stricter test is the right one.
+         */
+#if QJS_DRC
+        if (JS_REF_COUNT(JS_VALUE_GET_PTR(v)) < 1 &&
+            !JS_GC_IN_ZCT(JS_VALUE_GET_PTR(v))) {
+#else
         if (JS_REF_COUNT(JS_VALUE_GET_PTR(v)) < 1) {
+#endif
             fprintf(stderr,
                     "drc-validate: register %p[%u] holds a counted value at "
                     "ref_count %u\n",
@@ -9828,6 +9843,10 @@ static void js_drc_zero(JSRuntime *rt, JSValue v)
             JSGCObjectHeader *p = JS_VALUE_GET_PTR(v);
             list_del(&p->link);
             list_add(&p->link, &rt->gc_zero_ref_count_list);
+            /* Parked objects carry the same marker as parked strings, so a
+               borrowing register that still names one is not mistaken for a
+               dangling value (see js_drc_validate_range). */
+            JS_GC_IN_ZCT(p) = 1;
             rt->zct_nobj++;
         }
         break;
@@ -9901,6 +9920,7 @@ static void js_drc_reconcile_locked(JSRuntime *rt)
         p = list_entry(el, JSGCObjectHeader, link);
         if (JS_REF_COUNT(p) > 0) {
             list_del(&p->link);
+            JS_GC_IN_ZCT(p) = 0;   /* resurrected: no longer parked */
             if (JS_GC_NOTRACE(p))
                 list_add_tail(&p->link, &rt->gc_notrace_list);
             else
